## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates building a pluggable scheduling system where policies evolve without changing the core orchestrator. Focus on isolation, runtime extensibility, and safe concurrent access.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern for interchangeable scheduling algorithms
- Registry pattern for runtime policy registration
- Thread-safe, lazy-loaded registry and policy implementations
- Handling of job metadata for policy selection

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Job {
  id: string
  priority: number
  deadline?: Date
  resources?: any
}

interface SchedulingPolicy {
  pickNext(jobs: Job[]): Job | null;
}

class PolicyRegistry {
  private policies = new Map<string, SchedulingPolicy>();

  register(name: string, policy: SchedulingPolicy): void {
    this.policies.set(name, policy);
  }

  get(name: string): SchedulingPolicy | undefined {
    return this.policies.get(name);
  }
}

// FIFO Policy
class FIFOPolicy implements SchedulingPolicy {
  pickNext(jobs: Job[]): Job | null {
    return jobs.length > 0 ? jobs[0] : null;
  }
}

// Priority Policy
class PriorityPolicy implements SchedulingPolicy {
  pickNext(jobs: Job[]): Job | null {
    return jobs.sort((a, b) => b.priority - a.priority)[0] || null;
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new PolicyRegistry();
registry.register('fifo', new FIFOPolicy());
registry.register('priority', new PriorityPolicy());

const policy = registry.get('priority');
const nextJob = policy?.pickNext(jobQueue);
```

